@0 = ~Arbolon~

@1 = ~Welcome. Forgive my intrusion, but have you seen a gnome, this tall, brown hair and beard, absolutely insane and delusional? He believes he is meant for greater things, whereas he is nothing more than a common thief.~
@2 = ~Can't say that we have.~
@3 = ~I can't help you.~
@4 = ~I understand. Well, have a nice time. Sorry I bothered you.~

@5 = ~Welcome, travelers. My name is Arbolon. I'm pleased to... Tiax?!~
@6 = ~Arbo... I mean, who are you?! How dare you speak to Tiax without asking permission first!!~
@7 = ~You little scoundrel. First, you manipulate me and my brother, then you disappear with our wealth and now you have the audacity not to recognise me?!~
@8 = ~Tiax does not need to take liberties with witless oafs. Tiax's destiny is to rule all Toril and beyond, not waste time for idle chats with ciphers.~
@9 = ~A cipher?! You must be talking about yourself because I have never seen anyone more pitiful and insane than you, gnome! Where is the gold you stole from us?~
@10 = ~Hold on. Who are you again?~
@11 = ~How do you know Tiax?~
@12 = ~Back off! He is with me now.~

@13 = ~My name is Arbolon and I'm Ilmater's priest, from Athkatla. And HE is a blight on society! This gnome you're mindlessly befriending is seriously sick in his mind. I wouldn't make the mistake of underestimating him, lest you want to wake up one morning with your gold purses gone for good. Seventy-eight thousand gold he took! Seventy-eight thousand!~
@14 = ~How do you know Tiax?~

@15 = ~Tiax isn't even his real name, as much as I am aware. He used to 'live' in Athkatla, if you can call it that, and tricked me into thinking he was in need of aid. We accepted him into our temple and fed him. He played his part well, little runt. He made us believe he was poor and hungry, and preyed upon us for weeks.~
@16 = ~Some priests noticed that food, water and clothes were disappearing. We thought that maybe some other homeless commoners had taken them, but eventually I was the one to catch this little bastard stealing from the lockbox. Before I could stop him, he vanished into the night, leaving only the trail of my stinking failure. All the money we collected was gone in a matter of seconds because he somehow knew the location of the lockbox.~
@17 = ~No safe places for Cyric's chosen one. He will find anything, anywhere.~
@18 = ~Listen to me, traveller. Tiax suffers from delusional narcissism, and the most malignant kind. He seems harmless, but I guarantee you that if we don't cure it, it will get worse. He isn't a disciple of Cyric or whoever he claims himself to be... he is simply an ill individual with a fragile ego. Push him too hard and he will explode.~
@19 = ~As a priest of the Crying God I may be able to help him. I may know a ritual - a form of 'Cleansing,' if you will. If it goes well, I may be able to purge the thoughts of Cyric from Tiax's mind and break their hold over him.~

@22 = ~Your gold is of no concern to me. Tiax needs wealth to ascend, and since he had none at the time, he had to use any means necessary to acquire it. You should be thankful that an authority as wise as Tiax was responsible for the theft, not some random whackjob or beggar.~
@23 = ~Thankful?!! This money was collected for Ilmater priests, so we could build a church for those in need. Seventy-eight thousand gold coins we spent years collecting! Where is it, you filth?!~
@24 = ~Tis disrespectful to take money off someone as impoverished as Tiax, don't you think? Tiax has no money but he does have a privilege, and with such you shall treat me. Beg me for mercy and forgiveness, preacher!~
@25 = ~Where is the catch?~
@26 = ~Well... if it doesn't go well, the consequences could be grave. He could lose all his memories, his personality could change drastically, or... well, there is a plethora of possibilities I'd rather not discuss. My job as a priest of Ilmater is to aid narrow-minded individuals who need help, despite the bad they've done. And he might not be beyond saving yet.~
@27 = ~If there is a chance we can make him normal, sure. We're up.~
@28 = ~Nah. I think he's more fun that way. His delusions make for great comic relief in the group.~

@29 = ~Phenomenal. Tell me when you are ready and I'll start the chant. Luckily, we are in the middle of the wilderness, so no one should disrupt us.~
@30 = ~Tiax finds it amusing. Let's play your game, preacher. It won't do you any good but will result in Tiax continuing his ascension.~
@31 = ~Your cooperation is greatly appreciated. Once I begin chanting, I need you to stay completely still, Tiax. Do not move, do not speak, and do not touch anything. Whatever you hear, remain still. Can you do that?~
@32 = ~Tiax agrees.~
@33 = ~Splendid. Here we go.~

@34 = ~Atna...~
@35 = ~Potentis...~
@36 = ~Abra...~
@37 = ~Nevusima...~
@38 = ~(Tiax slaps his neck.) Damn mosquitos make Tiax very annoyed...~
@39 = ~NO, TIAX!!~

@40 = ~See? Tiax knew his destiny is to be Cyric's chosen one. The preacher was amusing, but his stupidity was astounding. Tiax is no fool. Tiax knows he will rule, as his visions make it obvious. Now, make some room. Tiax is walking here.~
@41 = ~Over my dead body! The slums of Athkatla haven't taught you any manners, and Cyric has driven you insane. Your sick beliefs are absolutely tarnishing your mind!~
